
# 2026-03-20 10:14:03.421994
+BUG DESCRIPTION --------------- Actual Behavior: GET /items/{id} raises an uncaught KeyError and results in a 500 crash when the item does not exist. GET /items/{id}/total raises a ZeroDivisionError and returns 500 when the item's quantity is 0. DELETE /items/{id} can raise KeyError for missing items and, on successful deletion, returns no response body or explicit status confirmation. Expected Behavior: Endpoints should handle missing resources and invalid input gracefully: GET /items/{id} should return 404 with a clear error body when the item is not found. GET /items/{id}/total should validate quantity and return 400 for invalid calculations (eg quantity 0) instead of crashing. DELETE /items/{id} should delete the resource when present and return a proper status (204 No Content or 200 with confirmation) without unhandled exceptions. Affected Area: Items API endpoints: GET /items/{id}, GET /items/{id}/total, DELETE /items/{id} ASSUMPTIONS ----------- - ⚠️ Backend is implemented in Python and unhandled dict lookups or arithmetic lead to KeyError and ZeroDivisionError respectively. - ⚠️ Expected API semantics: 404 for missing resources, 400 for client-side invalid input, and 204/200 for successful deletes; tests and validation currently missing. ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA ------------------- 1. Given a non-existent item id, when a client requests GET /items/{id}, then the service returns 404 with a JSON error body like {'error': 'item not found'} and no unhandled exception is raised. 2. Given an item with quantity 0, when a client requests GET /items/{id}/total, then the service returns 400 Bad Request with a JSON error body like {'error': 'invalid quantity for total'} and no ZeroDivisionError occurs. 3. Given an existing item id, when a client requests DELETE /items/{id}, then the service deletes the item, returns 204 No Content (or 200 with a confirmation JSON), and does not raise KeyError; no regression in item listing or creation endpoints should occur.
+
+  Instructions:
+  - Read the repo map and find the relevant files yourself.
+  - Make the minimal, targeted change needed.
+  - Do not refactor unrelated code.
+  - Do not create new files unless necessary.
+  

